data.content:
**No Thread.Abort migration is needed here.**

Your code only uses `Thread.Start`, `Thread.Join`, and `Thread.Sleep` — none of these are affected by the .NET 6+ breaking change. This code will compile and run correctly as-is on .NET 8.

---

**Optional modernization** (unrelated to Thread.Abort migration): You could simplify this with `Parallel.ForEach` or `Task.Run`, which gives you cleaner parallelism and built-in `CancellationToken` support if you need it later:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public void RunBatch(List<WorkItem> items, CancellationToken token = default)
    {
        Parallel.ForEach(items, new ParallelOptions { CancellationToken = token }, item =>
        {
            Process(item);
        });
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item)
    {
        Thread.Sleep(100);
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

But again — your existing code works fine on .NET 8 without any changes.
